  "lens": {
   "mechanics": "When spending financed by money and credit grows faster than the goods and services sold, prices rise. Gold pays no interest, so higher bond yields after inflation increase the cost of holding it.",
   "data": "Three forces matter. October consumer prices rose <a href=\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/cpi.nr0.htm\">0.4% on the month</a>, keeping pressure on policy. The last cash-market real yield was <a href=\"https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/TextView?type=daily_treasury_real_yield_curve&amp;field_tdr_date_value_month=202611\">2.78% on 10 Nov</a>, which raised the return investors give up when holding gold. Ten-year breakeven inflation was 2.20% on 10 Nov, below its prior reading. In a debt-monetisation episode real returns can fall as prices outrun yields. Here real yields rose in the last open Treasury session, while Wednesday’s gold decline coincided with a stronger dollar.",
   "unsustainable": "Persistent debt growth above revenue growth squeezes future spending. Spending cuts, restructuring, transfers and monetary expansion can each shift the cost of adjustment.",
